## Follow-up — FactoryTable synchronous manager-removal contention

- The pinned Java fixture update (`8734cca5`) invokes `removeClientFactory(id, instance)` concurrently with `computeIfPresent`. It observes that identity-safe removal waits for the callback, that the callback runs once, and that the computed replacement stays mapped. Fixture hash: `7c84370c42228a4f3676fde47af11656a3f20ab545d036b79febf13338b9909a`; pinned offline Maven verification passed.
- Added the native race regression first and confirmed it failed because synchronous manager removal bypassed the async remapping gate. Commit `c00fa4ec` replaces the dynamic async gate map with 256 fixed hash shards, each with async and synchronous exclusion. FactoryTable writes/remaps take both gates; synchronous identity-safe removal shares the synchronous gate. The entries mutex remains free while callbacks run. A non-poisoning, reentrant synchronous gate recovers on callback unwind; a same-key post-panic write test verifies recovery.
- Adjusted the existing `replaceAll` differential sequence to retain the manager-ID mapping present in the Java fixture before that operation. The focused manager table suite passes all 6 tests.
- Independent compatibility documentation commit: `415c1215` updates ADR-0080, the Phase 6 API map, requirement audit, and runtime inventory to record the synchronous removal race and fixed hash-shard semantics.
- Final gates pass: `cargo test --workspace --all-targets --locked`; strict all-target/all-feature Clippy; `cargo fmt --all -- --check`; `git diff --check`; pinned offline Maven verify (22 scenarios); `python3 tests/differential/compare.py --self-test`; and `--validate-manifest` (22 scenarios). Linux production runtime execution remains unavailable; the full Phase 6 API/source/input/platform reconciliation is still partial, and P6-V04 stays assigned to Phase 19.

